{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1248 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ecc8d66f1d57c0fb6208add3e9d65ed977699e1f131e3c36e2f7acf4569aa1e9" :edb/db-before-t 1247 :edb/db-after-t 1248 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534560 50 #inst "2026-10-08T00:56:49.278Z" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059533 1100 "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$." 13194139534560 false] [17592186059533 1100 "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059533 1145 "The range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/209/Source/Images/14075e642ddf4d45f6ee40cea7dbb087.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139534560 false] [17592186059533 1145 "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059534 1100 "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3)$." 13194139534560 false] [17592186059534 1100 "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059534 1145 "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139534560 false] [17592186059534 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059544 1100 "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right)$." 13194139534560 false] [17592186059544 1100 "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059544 1145 "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/209/Source/Images/cbdba1490f8dd76396ad73dfea0fe152.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ndoes not lie within the range of $\\theta$. To get an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand lies within the range of $\\theta$, we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139534560 false] [17592186059544 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186059735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295381 13194139534560 true] [17592186059735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295395 13194139534560 true] [17592186059735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295402 13194139534560 true] [17592186059735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295424 13194139534560 true] [17592186059736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295367 13194139534560 true] [17592186059736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295374 13194139534560 true] [17592186059736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295388 13194139534560 true] [1759218605973