refore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186269035 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534560 false] [17592186278584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295405 13194139534560 true] [17592186295360 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295361 13194139534560 true] [17592186295360 1089 "q-117408" 13194139534560 true] [17592186295360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534560 true] [17592186295360 1100 "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" 13194139534560 true] [17592186295360 1113 #uuid "1796fbf1-69e9-550e-8c0e-6de576d0b219" 13194139534560 true] [17592186295360 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{